WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.534
-Oh, it's good
to see you, buddy.

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.200
-It's been a long time
since you've been by the show.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.367
I'm happy to see you.
-It's great to see you, too.

00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:06.667
But I love your beard.

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:07.834
-You like it?
-Yeah.

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:09.667
I think it really suits you,
and I dig it.

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:11.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.834 --> 00:00:13.033
-Coming from you,
I appreciate it,

00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:14.634
'cause I want to
talk about your --

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:16.167
You have a great mustache
in this movie.

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:17.400
-[ Laughs ] Yes.
-Look at this.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.000
Talk about facial hair.
I mean, look at that.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.434
-Yeah.
-That's a real one.

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:22.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:23.601
-Ah, It was actually
fun to grow.

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:25.267
The director looked to me
on the first day,

00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:26.734
he goes, "That's real?"

00:00:26.734 --> 00:00:28.100
Yeah,
-Yeah, exactly.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.434
-He was even --
-That's when you know it's good.

00:00:29.434 --> 00:00:30.634
When somebody goes
"That's not real, right?"

00:00:30.634 --> 00:00:31.701
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:32.701
People with real beards
look at me,

00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:34.033
They go, "Nice try, Jimmy."

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.367
Yeah, you're going for it.

00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:38.067
I congratulate you, by the way.

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.634
Last time --
Since last time I saw you,

00:00:39.634 --> 00:00:41.400
you won an Emmy
for "Down to Earth."

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.834
-Right.
-Your show on Netflix.

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:44.234
-Thanks, man. Thank you.

00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:46.167
They love it.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.901
Here's one of the reasons
I love you.

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:50.868
You're very talented.
You can do everything.

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:52.567
But you're very adventurous.

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:53.834
When you do these things,

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:56.467
You do stuff that
I wouldn't find fun at all.

00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:59.767
But I like watching you do it
because it's exciting.

00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:02.167
You like swimming with
crocodiles and stuff like that

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:04.567
and bungee jumping.
-Yep.

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:06.334
Stuff that -- I couldn't do
any of that stuff.

00:01:06.334 --> 00:01:08.167
-Yeah, I mean, it all kind of
makes you nervous

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:09.167
before you do it.

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:10.467
-Is there anything
you wouldn't do?

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:12.701
-You know, I guess.
On swimming with sharks.

00:01:12.701 --> 00:01:14.701
I did swim with tiger sharks
out in Hawaii,

00:01:14.701 --> 00:01:17.033
and I love the people
that I went with,

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:20.767
but they invited me to go free
diving with Great White sharks,

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:23.033
and I just -- I was like,
why on earth would I do that?

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:24.634
That's just asking for it.
-Oh, good.

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:26.334
So I drew the line
-I'm so happy you drew the line!

00:01:26.334 --> 00:01:27.534
Yeah, you're like, hey,
I'm an American treasure.

00:01:27.534 --> 00:01:29.067
I'm Zac Efron.
I'm not doing this.

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:30.167
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:32.200
There's a big downside.
I didn't want to.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.968
That'd be a disappointing
way to go.

00:01:33.968 --> 00:01:35.100
-Can I ask you about --

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.734
There's a rumor --

00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:38.767
There's some casting calls
in the Internet.

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:41.267
Marvels looking
for a Zac Efron type.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.567
-Really?

00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:44.267
Wow, they could just call me.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:45.234
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, I know.

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:46.534
I was going to say..

00:01:46.534 --> 00:01:48.033
if you don't want to do it.

00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:50.334
And then I thought
it'd be funny.

00:01:50.334 --> 00:01:53.234
Seth Rogen, who we love,
tweeted out.

00:01:54.634 --> 00:01:57.968
"Crap, I was gonna submit myself
for the Zac Efron type."

00:01:57.968 --> 00:01:59.400
-[ Laughs ]
That's hilarious

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.501
-Isn't that funny
that he would do it?

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:01.767
-That's really funny, yeah.

00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:04.701
-Could Seth play you
a Zac Efron type, you think?

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:06.067
Seth? I think, yeah.

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:07.467
Seth could do it,
If anybody could.

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:10.467
He would -- I would cast Seth
in anything over me, so...

00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:11.834
-Yeah.
Yeah.

00:02:11.834 --> 00:02:13.434
-Have you been
ever approached by Marvel?

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:14.567
It was just -- That was funny.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.033
They were saying
a Zac Efron type, but you...

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:18.667
There was a rumor that
you were going to be

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.334
Wolverine at one point.

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:21.868
-Really? Dang.
-Yes! The Internet.

00:02:21.868 --> 00:02:23.033
-I don't know.
-The people want this.

00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:25.334
-I think Hugh's still doing
that pretty well.

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:27.300
I got to give it to him.
-Yeah, but you know Hugh.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.267
You were in
"The Greatest Showman."

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:30.467
-Yeah, you're right.

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.968
But that was a different movie
with musical.

00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:34.734
He'll let you do it.
-Okay, yeah.

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:36.300
Yeah, he's -- you know him?
He's a good guy.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.934
-Yeah, I guess I had my foot
in the door on that one.

00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:39.834
-I should go talk to him.
-Why not be Wolverine?

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:40.934
I would love to see you
as Wolverine.

00:02:40.934 --> 00:02:42.033
I can see it.
-Yeah,

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:44.701
I would get a beard like yours.
-Yes!

00:02:44.701 --> 00:02:45.767
I'll give you my beard.

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:47.367
I'll shave it off
and you can have my...

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:48.567
-All right.
-...clippings.

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:49.601
-If it happens.
-Let's talk about it.

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:50.601
Yeah, all right.

00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:51.601
Let's talk
about your movie,

00:02:51.601 --> 00:02:53.200
"The Greatest Beer Run Ever."

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.300
This is a true story?

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.367
And I almost can't believe
that it is a true story.

00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:59.100
-Yeah, it's really wild, man.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.133
So it all really happens.

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.501
Chickie Donohue is this guy
lived in a small town

00:03:04.501 --> 00:03:07.267
in Inwood, New York,
when he was younger,

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:10.200
and all of his friends
were all fighting in Vietnam,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.901
and he was just
sort of stuck at home

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:14.868
and he felt really powerless
to help them.

00:03:14.868 --> 00:03:16.801
And simultaneously
there was a lot of bad press

00:03:16.801 --> 00:03:18.133
coming through about Vietnam

00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:20.100
and sort of the
anti-war movement.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.601
And he just wanted to show
some support for his friends.

00:03:23.601 --> 00:03:27.601
So he decided to get
a duffel bag full of beer

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:30.734
and actually go
to the Vietnam front line

00:03:30.734 --> 00:03:33.467
and deliver each of
his enlisted friends a beer

00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:35.000
just to say thank you.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.634
-That's the craziest thing ever.

00:03:36.634 --> 00:03:38.467
It's a real story.
And he's a real person.

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:39.667
-Yes.
-Here he is.

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:40.667
-It really happened.
-Here's you and Chickie

00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:41.767
right here.

00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:43.067
-Yeah. And it gets
a lot crazier than that,

00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:45.701
as you can imagine.
He gets into some high jinks.

00:03:45.701 --> 00:03:48.634
-Oh, my gosh. And it's directed
by the great Peter Farrelly.

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:50.000
-Yeah, Pete Ferrell's in it.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.100
I mean, war is pretty sensitive
subject matter, you know,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.767
It's like we took it
really seriously,

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:56.100
and Pete has this amazing way

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.133
of going through
really kind of tough,

00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:02.634
you know, situations and sort
of serious subject matter

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:05.667
with this amazing
underlying humor.

00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:07.300
-It's kind of unbelievable.
When you watch,

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.701
you go like, wait, someone
actually did this.

00:04:08.701 --> 00:04:11.834
He went to Vietnam and he's
giving beers to his buddies.

00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:13.767
You're like, this can't be real.

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:15.934
-I know. I kept like I was
reading the script.

00:04:15.934 --> 00:04:17.033
I was like, "what the [ Bleep ]"

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:18.701
It was Bill Murray and Russell

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:20.400
and Russell Crowe.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.133
I mean, doing a movie with Bill
Murray and Russell Crowe,

00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:23.367
how fun was that?

00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:25.467
-It doesn't really get better
than that, man.

00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:26.801
-It really doesn't, right?

00:04:26.801 --> 00:04:28.767
-Yeah, I like both of those guys
for different reasons.

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:30.367
They're huge heroes of mine.

00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:33.234
But Bill Murray,
when he was on set,

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.968
I just I remember
just thinking to myself,

00:04:35.968 --> 00:04:39.234
I was like, I don't know
what's better than this.

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:40.601
-How about Russell.

00:04:40.601 --> 00:04:43.167
-And Russell's very,
very similar experience.

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:46.133
Russell came into the movie
sort of toward the end

00:04:46.133 --> 00:04:47.334
of the Vietnam section,

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:49.200
which we were shooting
in Thailand,

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.267
and he has
a pretty important role.

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:53.868
He plays this, like,
war photographer

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:55.801
that looks after Chickie
while he's there

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:58.434
on this really dumb mission.

00:04:58.434 --> 00:05:02.734
And so a lot of the scenes
that he's in are sort of action

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:06.267
were running through Thailand,
explosions, just heavy drama,

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:08.767
a lot of, you know, destruction
and stuff like that.

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:12.267
And we -- he came in
just so prepared.

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:13.834
He knows so much
about this craft

00:05:13.834 --> 00:05:15.634
that, like,
he could be directing it.

00:05:15.634 --> 00:05:17.367
He directs a lot of stuff.
-Of course.

00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:19.734
-But he also knows acting
so well,

00:05:19.734 --> 00:05:22.133
you just want to be next to him.

00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:23.567
-I know you're good,

00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:25.701
but you ask him for advice
when you're doing a scene?

00:05:25.701 --> 00:05:27.167
Do you go, like, hey,
would you do this?

00:05:27.167 --> 00:05:29.901
-Yes, a lot.

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:31.701
You can't help it,
I mean, he's Russell Crowe.

00:05:31.701 --> 00:05:33.200
He's like the best actor
that's ever lived.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.767
So I'm constantly
looking at him like.

00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:37.000
-Was that good?
-Is that all right?

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.067
-Yeah, exactly.

00:05:38.067 --> 00:05:39.434
-How was that?

00:05:39.434 --> 00:05:41.601
There was one scene that we had.

00:05:41.601 --> 00:05:44.200
It was kind of a monologue
that my character has

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.300
just, like, a memory,
and he's kind of going into it,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.000
you know, these old thoughts.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.567
And, and this was
like four days into the shoot.

00:05:51.567 --> 00:05:53.033
So I had gotten to know
Russell pretty well.

00:05:53.033 --> 00:05:55.033
-Yeah.
-We were friends at this point,

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:57.100
and I did it once,

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.567
and I kind of looked
at him like,

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:01.367
"Anything you can fix?"
And he's like,

00:06:01.367 --> 00:06:02.701
No, no, it's good.

00:06:02.701 --> 00:06:04.467
Did another take,
and it was like,

00:06:04.467 --> 00:06:06.000
"Anything?
Like, you can tell me?"

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.601
And he's like,
No, no, it's good.

00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:09.634
Did one more and I went,
"Come on, give me som--"

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:11.200
And he goes.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.434
"Okay, stop moving your
[ Bleep ] hands.

00:06:15.434 --> 00:06:16.601
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:18.467
And I was like, "Oh, really?"

00:06:18.467 --> 00:06:20.400
And I never thought back
to the past takes.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:21.701
And I was going like,

00:06:21.701 --> 00:06:23.467
I thought this was a part
of my New York character.

00:06:23.467 --> 00:06:24.734
I was just going like.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.000
Yeah. Like, this is how I feel

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.000
and all these things and ahh.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.400
Like that.
-Stop moving your hands.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.167
-And he said that
and I was like, I took it.

00:06:33.167 --> 00:06:34.367
-Wow.
-I was cracking up.

00:06:34.367 --> 00:06:35.534
It was the best note ever.

00:06:35.534 --> 00:06:37.667
The next one, I literally
just locked my hands

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:39.367
in my waist.

00:06:39.367 --> 00:06:40.400
Like, just right here.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.501
Just wouldn't let him go.

00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:42.901
And we did one more take.

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:43.968
And after that,
everybody was like,

00:06:43.968 --> 00:06:45.968
"Yes! We got it!

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:47.100
We got it!
-And he's like,

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.300
"You got it, kid."

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:49.667
-Russell was just like,
"There you go, kid."

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:50.934
-"Here you go, kid."
-So that's the kind of guy --

00:06:50.934 --> 00:06:52.400
-It's a fantastic film.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.067
And of course, Peter Farrelly
just coming off his Oscar win.

00:06:55.067 --> 00:06:56.067
-Yep, yep.
-This is it.

00:06:56.067 --> 00:06:57.234
You got to check out this movie.

00:06:57.234 --> 00:06:59.234
-And you know, you know,
you worked back on --

00:06:59.234 --> 00:07:01.567
"Fever Pitch"
with the Farrelly brothers.

00:07:01.567 --> 00:07:02.701
Unreal. He told me to say
hello to you, man.

00:07:02.701 --> 00:07:04.033
-He's the greatest, greatest.

00:07:04.033 --> 00:07:05.234
I loved both of you guys.
-He is. Honestly, the best.

00:07:05.234 --> 00:07:07.434
You guys gotta check this out.
Here's a clip.

00:07:07.434 --> 00:07:09.701
Here's Zac Efron in
"The Greatest Beer Run Ever."

00:07:09.701 --> 00:07:10.801
Take a look.

00:07:10.801 --> 00:07:12.300
[ Gun fire ]

00:07:18.434 --> 00:07:19.767
[ Breathing heavily ]

00:07:21.534 --> 00:07:22.667
-You wanted to see me Sarge?

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:23.667
-No.

00:07:26.701 --> 00:07:28.367
-Ta-da!

00:07:28.367 --> 00:07:29.934
[ Laughter ]

00:07:29.934 --> 00:07:31.000
-Chickie?

00:07:32.467 --> 00:07:33.801
What are you doing here?

00:07:33.801 --> 00:07:35.200
-I brung you beer.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.067
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.067 --> 00:07:38.400
-Who -- Who are you with?

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.868
-I'm with you.

00:07:39.868 --> 00:07:41.000
-Seriously, Chickie.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.334
What the hell
are you doing here?

00:07:42.334 --> 00:07:43.567
Really?

00:07:43.567 --> 00:07:45.734
-Me and all the fellows
we're sitting around.

00:07:45.734 --> 00:07:47.734
talking about what we could do
to bring your spirits up.

00:07:47.734 --> 00:07:49.367
But then, Bang! It hit us.

00:07:49.367 --> 00:07:50.868
Bring you a beer.

00:07:50.868 --> 00:07:52.234
So I brought you a beer.

00:07:53.167 --> 00:07:54.968
-You dumb [ No audio ]

00:07:54.968 --> 00:07:56.434
-What?

00:07:56.434 --> 00:07:58.667
-You just got me killed
out there!

00:07:58.667 --> 00:07:59.467
-That's what I'm talking about.
Come on, bud.

00:07:59.467 --> 00:08:02.868
Zac Efron, everybody.

00:08:02.868 --> 00:08:05.300
"The Greatest Beer Run Ever"

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.934
is streaming now on Apple TV.

00:08:07.934 --> 00:08:09.934
Plus, and in select theaters.

00:08:09.934 --> 00:08:11.901
We'll be right back
with Debbie Harry.

